    <h1>How Do Ravens Build Nests? Materials and Construction</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens build large stick platforms on cliff ledges or in tall tree forks, lined with wool, hair, bark, and grass. Both members of the pair contribute to construction, which takes 1–3 weeks for a new nest. Ravens reuse and add to the same nest site year after year; long-established nests can exceed 1 m across.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Structure</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> A raven nest has a characteristic two-layer architecture:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Outer platform:</strong> large sticks and branches, some 30–60 cm in length, interlocked to form a stable foundation. The platform may be placed on a cliff ledge, in the fork of a tree, or (more rarely) on a man-made structure such as a pylon or bridge girder.</li>
      <li><strong>Inner cup (lining):</strong> finer, softer materials woven into the stick platform — wool (from sheep or other domestic animals), animal hair, dried grass, strips of bark, root fibres, and plant down. The cup is shaped to hold the eggs and provide insulation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A fresh nest measures roughly 50–80 cm in diameter and 20–40 cm deep. After years of additions, nests can reach over a metre across and become structurally massive.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Construction Behaviour</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens begin nest construction (or refurbishment) weeks before egg-laying — typically 4–6 weeks before the first egg. The process:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The male typically collects large structural sticks, often breaking them from live or dead trees or finding them on the ground.</li>
      <li>The female does much of the placement and weaving, shaping the cup by turning in the nest and pressing outward with her breast.</li>
      <li>Lining materials — especially wool and hair — are collected from the surrounding landscape. In areas with sheep, ravens actively gather wool from fence wire, rocks, and recently shorn sheep.</li>
      <li>A new nest takes approximately 1–3 weeks to complete; refurbishing an existing nest may take days.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Site Fidelity and Nest Reuse</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens show strong nest-site fidelity — pairs return to the same location year after year. Each season they add fresh sticks and lining. This cumulative construction produces some of the largest bird nests in temperate regions. Pairs may maintain 2–3 alternate nest sites within their territory, using different sites in different years or in response to disturbance.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

